{"cuisine/antigua-and-barbuda-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/antigua-and-barbuda-cuisine","n":"Antigua and Barbuda cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Antigua and Barb.","intro":[["Antigua and Barbuda cuisine refers to the cuisines of the Caribbean islands Antigua and Barbuda. The national dish is fungee and pepperpot. Fungee is a dish similar to Italian polenta, made mostly with cornmeal. Other local dishes include ducana, seasoned rice, saltfish and lobster (from Barbuda). There are also local confectioneries which include sugar cake, fudge, raspberry and tamarind stew, and peanut brittle."],["These foods are indigenous to Antigua and Barbuda and to some other Caribbean countries, the local diet has diversified and includes local dishes of Jamaica, such as jerk meats, or Trinidad, such as roti, and specialties of other Caribbean countries."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"antigua-and-barbuda-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antigua_and_Barbuda_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/c2/Jerk_chicken_july_05.jpg/500px-Jerk_chicken_july_05.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Unknown author","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jerk_chicken_july_05.jpg"}},"cuisine/bhojpuri-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/bhojpuri-cuisine","n":"Bhojpuri cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["North Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-indian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Bhojpuri cuisine is a style of food preparation common among the Bhojpuri people of Bihar, Jharkhand and eastern Uttar Pradesh in India, and also the Terai region of Nepal. Bhojpuri foods are mostly mild and tend to be less hot in terms of spices used. The cuisine consists of both vegetable and non-vegetarian dishes."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"bhojpuri-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bhojpuri_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["North Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-indian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Litti (dish)","dish/litti-dish"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Atta","ingredient/atta"],["Besan","ingredient/besan"],["Brinjal","ingredient/brinjal"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Cilantro","ingredient/cilantro"],["Ghee","ingredient/ghee"],["Gram flour","ingredient/gram-flour"],["Mustard oil","ingredient/mustard-oil"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Sattu","ingredient/sattu"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["North Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-indian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/55/Litti_Chokha.jpg/500px-Litti_Chokha.jpg","w":500,"h":318,"by":"Amrita Nityanand Singh","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Litti_Chokha.jpg","of":"Litti (dish)"}},"cuisine/cuisine-of-basilicata":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-basilicata","n":"Basilicatan (Lucanian) cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Italy","intro":[["The cuisine of Basilicata, or Lucanian cuisine, is the cuisine of the Basilicata region of Italy. It is mainly based on the use of pork and sheep meat, legumes, cereals and vegetables, with the addition of aromas such as hot peppers, powdered raw peppers and horseradish. The local gastronomy is, for historical-cultural reasons, typically peasant, based on simple recipes and on the culture of reuse, in particular of meat and bread."],["Some dishes have undergone variations and enrichments in modern times, losing the connotations of \"poor\" cooking which characterized them in the past. The most ancient manuscript available about Lucanian cooking dates back to 1524, by Antonio Camuria from Lagonegro, cook at the service of the Carafa family."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-basilicata"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Basilicata"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Inside the Rustic Kitchen: Basilicata recipes","ref":"site/insidetherustickitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"insidetherustickitchen.com","note":"Italian home-cooking blog's dedicated section of dishes from Basilicata.","url":"https://www.insidetherustickitchen.com/category/basilicata-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Tumact me tulez","dish/tumact-me-tulez"],["U' pastizz 'rtunnar","dish/u-pastizz-rtunnar"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Anchovies as food","ingredient/anchovies-as-food"],["Breadcrumbs","ingredient/breadcrumbs"],["Pasta","ingredient/pasta"],["Tagliatelle","ingredient/tagliatelle"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Walnut","ingredient/walnut"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/24/Tumact_me_tulez.jpg/500px-Tumact_me_tulez.jpg","w":500,"h":435,"by":"Generale Lee","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tumact_me_tulez.jpg","of":"Tumact me tulez"}},"cuisine/maltese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/maltese-cuisine","n":"Maltese cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Malta","intro":[["Maltese cuisine reflects Maltese history, showing strong Italian influences, as well as influences from Spanish, French, Provençal and other Mediterranean cuisines, with some later British culinary influence. Being positioned along important trade routes, having to import most of its foodstuffs, and having to cater to the resident foreign powers who ruled the islands opened Maltese cuisine to outside influences. The traditional Maltese stewed rabbit, stuffat tal-fenek, is often identified as the national dish."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"maltese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maltese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Malta: Mediterranean Recipes from the Islands","ref":"cookbook/malta-mediterranean-recipes-from-the-islands","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Simon Bajada, 2019","note":"Recipes and food culture of Malta and Gozo, published by Hardie Grant.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781743798812","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781743798812"},{"name":"Taste of Malta","ref":"cookbook/taste-of-malta","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Claudia M. Caruana","note":"Hippocrene collection of traditional Maltese recipes.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":4,"links":[["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Imqaret","dish/imqaret"],["Maltese bread","dish/maltese-bread"],["Pastizz","dish/pastizz"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Filo","ingredient/filo"],["Mushy peas","ingredient/mushy-peas"],["Puff pastry","ingredient/puff-pastry"],["Ricotta","ingredient/ricotta"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/f/fc/Malta_Pastizzi.JPG","w":500,"h":500,"by":"Chattacha","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malta_Pastizzi.JPG"}},"ingredient/smetana":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/smetana","n":"Smetana","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 7 dishes across 10 cuisines","intro":[["Smetana is the English-language name for the different types of sour cream traditionally prevalent in Central, Eastern, and Southeastern Europe, and Central Asia. It is a dairy product produced by souring heavy cream. It is similar to crème fraîche, but nowadays mainly sold with 9% to 42% milkfat content depending on the country. Its cooking properties are different from crème fraîche and sour cream sold in the US, which contain 18% butterfat. It is widely used in cooking and baking."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-smetana"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smetana_(dairy_product)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":10,"groups":[["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Beef Stroganoff","dish/beef-stroganoff"],["Karelian pasty","dish/karelian-pasty"],["Okroshka","dish/okroshka"],["Sorrel soup","dish/sorrel-soup"]]],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",[["Banosh","dish/banosh"],["Sorrel soup","dish/sorrel-soup"]]],["Belarusian cuisine","cuisine/belarusian-cuisine",[["Sorrel soup","dish/sorrel-soup"]]],["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Beef Stroganoff","dish/beef-stroganoff"]]],["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine",[["Chicken tabaka","dish/chicken-tabaka"]]],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Stracciatella (ice cream)","dish/stracciatella-ice-cream"]]],["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine",[["Sorrel soup","dish/sorrel-soup"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Beef Stroganoff","dish/beef-stroganoff"]]],["Polish cuisine","cuisine/polish-cuisine",[["Sorrel soup","dish/sorrel-soup"]]],["Soviet cuisine","cuisine/soviet-cuisine",[["Chicken tabaka","dish/chicken-tabaka"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Smetana (dairy product)","ingredient/smetana-dairy-product"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Sour cream","ingredient/sour-cream"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Bryndza","ingredient/bryndza"],["Cornmeal","ingredient/cornmeal"],["Maize flour","ingredient/maize-flour"],["Tomato paste","ingredient/tomato-paste"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Tkemali","ingredient/tkemali"],["Barley","ingredient/barley"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Hordeum","ingredient/hordeum"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a7/Borscht_served.jpg/500px-Borscht_served.jpg","w":500,"h":327,"by":"liz west from Boxborough, MA","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borscht_served.jpg"}},"ingredient/meju":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/meju","n":"Meju","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Meju is a brick of dried fermented soybeans. While not consumed on its own, it serves as the basis of several Korean condiments, such as doenjang (soybean paste), ganjang (soy sauce), and gochujang (chili paste). Meju is produced by pounding, kneading, and shaping cooked soybeans, and undergoes fermentation with Aspergillus oryzae and/or Bacillus subtilis."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-meju"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meju"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine",[["Doenjang","dish/doenjang"],["Gochujang","dish/gochujang"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Brine","ingredient/brine"],["Soybean","ingredient/soybean"],["Chili powder","ingredient/chili-powder"],["Glutinous rice","ingredient/glutinous-rice"],["Gochu-garu","ingredient/gochu-garu"],["Malt","ingredient/malt"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f8/Meju_%28fermented_soybean%29.jpg/500px-Meju_%28fermented_soybean%29.jpg","w":500,"h":332,"by":"mspphoto","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meju_(fermented_soybean).jpg"}},"ingredient/herring":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/herring","n":"Herring","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Herring are various species of forage fish, belonging to the order Clupeiformes."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-herring"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herring"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Dressed herring","dish/dressed-herring"],["Forshmak","dish/forshmak"]]],["Brandenburg cuisine","cuisine/brandenburg-cuisine",[["Forshmak","dish/forshmak"]]],["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine",[["Forshmak","dish/forshmak"]]],["Soviet cuisine","cuisine/soviet-cuisine",[["Dressed herring","dish/dressed-herring"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beetroot","ingredient/beetroot"],["Beta","ingredient/beta"],["Beta vulgaris","ingredient/beta-vulgaris"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Clupea","ingredient/clupea"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Mayonnaise","ingredient/mayonnaise"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"]]},{"h":"Mentioned in","links":[["Haida foodways","cuisine/haida-foodways"],["Nuu-chah-nulth foodways","cuisine/nuu-chah-nulth-foodways"],["Tlingit cuisine","cuisine/tlingit-cuisine"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/c5/Clupea_harengus_Gervais.flipped.jpg/500px-Clupea_harengus_Gervais.flipped.jpg","w":500,"h":165,"by":"Gervais et Boulart, 1877","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clupea_harengus_Gervais.flipped.jpg"}},"dish/evil-jungle-prince":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/evil-jungle-prince","n":"Evil jungle prince","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Evil jungle prince  is a Thai-inspired dish which was created in Hawaiʻi in 1977 by Laotian American Keo Sananikone for his Mekong restaurant in Honolulu.  Later versions by Sananikone included other proteins.  Sananikone also created the evil princess, but that is a cocktail and not a related dish."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-evil-jungle-prince"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evil_jungle_prince"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Hawaiian cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-hawaii"],["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Basil","ingredient/basil"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Tofu","ingredient/tofu"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Onolicious Hawaii","ref":"site/onolicioushawaii-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"onolicioushawaii.com","note":"Hawaii-based blog of local-style recipes.","url":"https://onolicioushawaii.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Hot Thai Kitchen","ref":"site/hot-thai-kitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"hot-thai-kitchen.com","note":"Pailin Chongchitnant's recipes and videos, with background on technique and ingredients.","url":"https://hot-thai-kitchen.com","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"SheSimmers","ref":"site/shesimmers-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"shesimmers.com","note":"Bangkok-born writer Leela Punyaratabandhu's blog on Thai home cooking.","url":"https://shesimmers.com/who-is-shesimmers","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Hawaiian cuisine","links":[["Butter mochi","dish/butter-mochi"],["Poi (Hawaiian food)","dish/poi-hawaiian-food"]]}]},"dish/karen-keygnaert":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/karen-keygnaert","n":"Karen Keygnaert","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Fusion cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Karen Keygnaert is a Belgian-born chef, who won a Michelin star at Restaurant A'Qi. She has since opened Cantine Copine, where she announced she would not be pursuing Michelin stars."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-karen-keygnaert"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karen_Keygnaert"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]]}]},"ingredient/levantine-cuisine":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/levantine-cuisine","n":"Levantine cuisine","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Levantine cuisine is the traditional cuisine of the Levant. The cuisine has similarities with other Eastern Mediterranean cuisines, such as Balkan cuisine, particularly Greek Egyptian cuisine, North African cuisine, and Ottoman cuisine. It is particularly known for its meze spreads of hot and cold dishes, most notably among them Manakish pies, kibbeh, ful medames, hummus, tabbouleh and baba ghanoush, accompanied by bread."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-levantine-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Levantine_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Palestinian cuisine","cuisine/palestinian-cuisine",[["Kibbeh","dish/kibbeh"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bulgur","ingredient/bulgur"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"]]}]},"ingredient/egg-wash":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/egg-wash","n":"Egg wash","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["An egg wash is beaten eggs, sometimes mixed with another liquid such as water or milk, which is brushed onto the surface of a pastry before baking or breading. Egg washes can also be used on calzones or on fish."]],"src"